# Third-party code shipped inside baxters-soc-ultralight

Everything SOC Ultralight needs comes from Ubuntu packages, declared in the
package's Depends, with two exceptions. Neither has a package in the Ubuntu
archive, both are pure Python, and both are therefore vendored in `vendor/`.

## mss 10.2.0 — MIT

Screen capture. Vendored verbatim; nothing here patches it.
Licence text: `vendor/LICENSE.mss.txt`.
Upstream: https://github.com/BoboTiG/python-mss

## pytesseract 0.3.13 — Apache License 2.0

A thin wrapper around the `tesseract` binary, which this package depends on as
`tesseract-ocr` rather than bundling. Vendored verbatim; nothing here patches it.
Licence text: `vendor/LICENSE.pytesseract.txt`.
Upstream: https://github.com/madmaze/pytesseract

## Why vendored rather than pip-installed

Until 2026-09-25 the launcher created a virtualenv and ran
`pip install -r requirements.txt` on first start. That reached the network at
start-up, which the packaging contract forbids, and could not work at all once
installed, because the install directory is root-owned. Vendoring two pure
Python modules removes the need for either.

Both licences are permissive and require only that the notice travel with the
code, which `vendor/LICENSE.*.txt` and this file satisfy.
